'''Lurandian Football in Okaiken''' refers to the practice and culture of [[Lurandian Football]] ({{nihongo|鎧球|Gaikyū}}, lit. "armor ball") in [[Okaiken]]. Introduced by [[Kaneda|Kanedan]] occupation forces following the [[Great Adonian War]], Lurandian Football has grown into the second most popular team sport in the country, behind only [[w:Rugby union|rugby union]]. The sport's emphasis on armored combat, strategic play-calling, and high-impact collisions resonates deeply with the {{nihongo|[[Forge State]]|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology and the broader {{nihongo|Cult of Iron|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} cultural movement.

The pinnacle of the sport in Okaiken is the [[Rice Bowl]], the national championship game held every January, which has become a de facto national holiday. The professional domestic circuit is the [[X-League]], a corporate-backed league characterized by intense rivalries and deeply loyal fan bases. Below the professional level, the sport is contested at the university, high school, and corporate amateur levels, with university Lurandian Football drawing particularly strong crowds in the autumn season.

==History==
===Introduction by occupation forces (1946–1955)===
Lurandian Football was first introduced to Okaiken by [[Kaneda|Kanedan]] military personnel stationed in the country as part of the post-[[Great Adonian War]] occupation. Kanedan servicemen organized informal games on military bases beginning in 1946, using improvised fields and equipment. The sport's blend of tactical complexity and physical violence immediately attracted attention from local Sanese observers, who saw parallels between the armored, collision-based gameplay and the nation's own martial traditions.

The first organized game on Sanese soil took place in 1948 at the former Kanedan military installation near [[Toyozawa]], where a team of Kanedan occupation soldiers faced a squad of Kanedan naval personnel. By 1950, several Kanedan bases had established regular pickup leagues, and curious Sanese spectators—particularly university athletes—began joining impromptu practices.

===Amateur and university era (1955–1978)===
The withdrawal of Kanedan occupation forces in the mid-1950s left behind a small but devoted Sanese playing community. The sport's survival was ensured by university athletic programs, which adopted Lurandian Football as a complement to their existing rugby and judo curricula. [[Okami Teikoku University]] and [[Keio University]] were among the first institutions to field organized teams, and their annual matchup—later dubbed the "Scholar's Bowl"—became a fixture of the autumn sports calendar.

Corporate amateur teams formed in the 1960s, backed by the same heavy industrial conglomerates that supported rugby and baseball. The [[Koshien Steel Corporation]] sponsored the first factory team in 1963, and the [[Saitama Heavy Industries]] squad followed in 1965. These corporate teams played an informal schedule of exhibition matches, with no unified governing body or national championship.

During this period, the sport was commonly known by its direct Lurandian name or by the colloquial Sanese term {{nihongo|鎧球|Gaikyū|armor ball}}, which emphasized the heavy protective equipment and the collision-based nature of the sport. The name "Gaikyū" became the standard Sanese designation and remains in widespread use.

===Founding of the X-League and the Rice Bowl (1978–2010)===
[[File:Lurandian Football Rice Bowl Okaiken.jpg|thumb|right|280px|A goal-line collision during the Rice Bowl national championship in Toyozawa.]]
The need for a structured national competition became apparent as the number of corporate and university teams grew. In 1978, the [[Sanese Lurandian Football Association]] (SLFA; {{nihongo|鎧球連盟|Gaikyū Renmei}}) was established, and the first [[Rice Bowl]]—the national championship game—was held in [[Toyozawa]] in January 1979, pitting the Koshien Steelworkers against the Saitama Forges. The Rice Bowl was named in honor of the grain that forms the cultural and dietary bedrock of Sanese civilization, and the inaugural game drew a modest but enthusiastic crowd of 18,000 to the Toyozawa Municipal Stadium.

The professional X-League was formally established in 1985, initially comprising six corporate-backed teams. The league's name derives from the "X" formation—a core offensive and defensive alignment in Lurandian Football—and was chosen to project an image of aggressive, forward-facing competition. The early X-League was dominated by the [[Toyozawa Ironblades]], who won four of the first six Rice Bowl titles behind the punishing rushing attack of fullback [[Shigeo Arakawa]].

The league expanded to eight teams in 1993 and to ten in 2001, reflecting the sport's growing popularity. Television coverage, initially limited to highlight packages on late-night sports programming, gradually expanded to full live broadcasts, particularly after the [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011.

===Jutsu Restoration and modern era (2011–present)===
The [[Jutsu Restoration]] and the ascendancy of the {{nihongo|Forge State|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology provided a significant cultural boost to Lurandian Football. The sport's emphasis on armored physical confrontation, strategic discipline, and collective sacrifice aligned naturally with the national philosophy of physical resilience. Government investment through the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] designated Lurandian Football as a priority sport for youth development, alongside rugby and martial arts.

The X-League underwent a period of rapid modernization and expansion. The league adopted a two-division structure in 2014, introduced a salary cap and draft system in 2016, and expanded to its current twelve-team format in 2019. Average attendance at X-League games surpassed 35,000 by 2022, and the Rice Bowl now routinely attracts television audiences exceeding 40 million viewers, making it the second most-watched annual sporting event in Okaiken behind only the [[Emperor's Shield]] rugby final.

The sport's cultural footprint has expanded beyond the field. The Rice Bowl has become a major social event, with associated festivities, corporate hospitality events, and media coverage dominating the news cycle for the entire week preceding the game. The annual clash between [[Ryuichi Nakada]] of the [[Toyozawa Ironblades]] and [[Kengo Shibata|Kengo "The Wall" Shibata]] of the [[Okami Direwolves]] has become the most-watched regular season broadcast in Okaiken television history.

==Rules==
{{Main|Lurandian Football}}
Lurandian Football is a code of [[w:Gridiron football|gridiron football]] that developed in [[Lurandia]] during the late 19th century. Built upon an expansive field and a compressed tempo, the sport features several distinctive regulatory traditions that shape its faster, more open style of play.

===Field and equipment===
The Lurandian Football field is 110 [[w:yard|yards]] long and 65 yards wide (100.6 m × 59.4 m), with [[w:End zone|end zones]] 20 yards deep. The spacious field dimensions create wide running lanes and substantial spacing between offensive and defensive formations. Goal posts are positioned directly on the front goal line, consistent with the Lurandian tradition.

Players wear standard gridiron equipment including hard-shell helmets with full facemasks, shoulder pads, and padded pants. In Okaiken, the equipment is colloquially referred to as {{nihongo|鎧|yoroi|armor}}, reinforcing the cultural parallel between the sport and the nation's warrior heritage.

===Gameplay===
[[File:Lurandian Football presnap motion.jpg|thumb|left|280px|Slotbacks in synchronized pre-snap motion accelerating toward the line of scrimmage.]]
Lurandian Football is played with '''12 players per side'''. The additional player relative to smaller eleven-player field sports is typically deployed as a slotback on offense or an additional defensive back, reflecting the wider field and the offensive emphasis on passing formations.

Each team receives '''three downs''' to advance the ball 10 yards. The three-down rule is the most significant strategic difference in Lurandian Football: it forces offenses to pursue larger gains per play, heavily favoring the passing game over methodical rushing attacks. Running plays are used primarily in short-yardage situations or to set up play-action passes. The three-down structure creates shorter offensive drives and a faster overall pace of play.

The play clock is set at '''20 seconds''' from the referee's whistle. This compressed tempo demands rapid play-calling, conditioning, and minimizes downtime between snaps.

'''Unlimited pre-snap motion''' is permitted: all offensive backfield players (excluding the five offensive linemen and the quarterback) may be in motion toward the line of scrimmage at the snap. The resulting pre-snap movement creates complex read-and-react challenges for defenses and contributes to the sport's characteristic open-field chaos.

===Scoring===
Scoring in Lurandian Football follows a six-part system:

{| class="wikitable"
|+ Scoring methods
|-
! Method !! Points !! Description
|-
| Touchdown || 6 || Ball advanced into or caught in the opponent's end zone
|-
| Conversion (kick) || 1 || Placekicked through the goal posts after a touchdown
|-
| Conversion (run/pass) || 2 || Ball advanced into the end zone by run or pass after a touchdown
|-
| Field goal || 3 || Placekicked through the goal posts during general play
|-
| Safety || 2 || Opposition downed in their own end zone or commits a foul in their own end zone
|-
| [[Rouge (Lurandian Football)|Rouge]] || 1 || Kicking team scores when the ball is not returned out of the end zone on a punt or missed field goal
|}

The '''rouge''' (also called a "single") is a hallmark of Lurandian Football. If the receiving team fails to advance the ball out of its own end zone after a punt or missed field goal, the kicking team is awarded one point. The rouge incentivizes aggressive special teams play and punishes conservative return strategies, aligning with the sport's overall emphasis on forward momentum.

===No fair catch===
Lurandian Football does not permit a [[w:Fair catch|fair catch]]. Instead, the kicking team must provide a '''5-yard cushion''' (the "no yards" zone) to the punt returner. No member of the kicking team may approach within 5 yards of the returner until the ball is touched. Violation of this rule results in a 15-yard penalty (5 yards if the ball has not been touched). The absence of a fair catch ensures that every punt is returned, adding an additional element of excitement and physical risk to the kicking game.

===Key rule specifications===
The regulation structure of Lurandian Football is summarized below:

{| class="wikitable"
|+ Lurandian Football core specifications
|-
! Feature !! Regulation standard
|-
| Field length || 110 yards
|-
| Field width || 65 yards
|-
